---
import type { TocItem } from '../consts';
import {
  CORE_NON_CLAIMS,
  NOMUE_POSITION,
  PAIRED_T_BOUNDARY,
  PAIRED_T_RFC_URL,
  PRODUCT_RELEASE_BOUNDARY,
  PUBLIC_RELEASE,
} from '../data/site-facts';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const toc: TocItem[] = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What nomue is' },
  { id: 'roles', label: 'Division of roles' },
  { id: 'call', label: 'How a call works' },
  { id: 'availability', label: 'Current availability' },
  { id: 'trust', label: 'Public trust layer' },
  { id: 'agent-readable', label: 'Agent-readable docs' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What it does not claim' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="nomue"
  description="nomue is Licklider's scientific verification product for bounded, agent-callable verification work."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">nomue</p>
    <h1>Scientific verification without asking the research model to judge itself</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      nomue is designed as a specialist capability that research agents can call for
      bounded statistical decisions and numerical checks. It is not another general
      research agent.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section id="what" class="section">
    <h2>What nomue is</h2>
    <p>{NOMUE_POSITION}</p>
    <p>
      The product question is deliberately narrow: given these research conditions and
      this calculation or result, may the result be relied on for this explicitly
      bounded meaning?
    </p>
    <p>
      The answer may be a supported execution or scoped verification result. It may also
      be a request for clarification, unsupported scope, inadmissibility, safe refusal,
      a failed check, or an operational failure. Those outcomes are kept separate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="roles" class="section">
    <h2>A deliberate division of roles</h2>
    <div class="sequence" aria-label="Research agent, nomue, and researcher roles">
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Research agent</p>
        <p>
          Understand the task, gather context, explain required declarations,
          orchestrate the call, and explain the returned evidence and reason codes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">nomue</p>
        <p>
          Apply the exact capability and version boundary, protect scientific
          declarations from silent inference, execute the registered deterministic
          path, and return scoped machine-readable outcomes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Researcher</p>
        <p>
          Supply scientific facts that cannot be inferred, resolve experimental meaning,
          and remain responsible for the research judgment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="call" class="section">
    <h2>How a verification call works</h2>
    <ol class="call-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Identify.</strong> The agent names the exact capability, version, and
        question rather than asking for generic correctness.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Declare.</strong> Required scientific facts are supplied by an authorized
        source or remain explicitly unresolved; the model does not fill them in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide.</strong> nomue determines whether the supported path can proceed,
        needs clarification, or must stop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check.</strong> A supported numerical answer comes from deterministic
        execution and the applicable verification contract, not generated prose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Return the boundary.</strong> The outcome includes evidence, versions,
        next actions, and what remains not asserted.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p><a href="/docs/verification-call/">Read the verification-call guide →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="availability" class="section">
    <h2>Current availability and support</h2>
    <div class="status-list">
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Public today</p>
        <h3>Protocol Release 1 and local verifier</h3>
        <p>{`${PUBLIC_RELEASE.protocol} is public and experimental. Its sole public Record interpretation support target covers ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.profile} under the ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.procedure}. The separate verifier package remains ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierMaturity}.`}</p>
        <p><a href="/docs/record-verification/">How to use the public verifier →</a></p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Product</p>
        <h3>Agent-callable Welch capability</h3>
        <p>{PRODUCT_RELEASE_BOUNDARY}</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Successor</p>
        <h3>Paired-t Release 2 RFC</h3>
        <p>{PAIRED_T_BOUNDARY}</p>
        <p><a href={PAIRED_T_RFC_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public RFC →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="trust" class="section">
    <h2>The public trust layer</h2>
    <p>
      The public meaning of a supported Record does not depend on trusting Licklider's
      product service. The nomue Protocol defines public Record and scoped verification
      semantics. The open-source verifier checks the covered properties locally and
      offline.
    </p>
    <p>
      The product layer remains separate. It can manage agent-facing intake,
      clarification, execution, and operational behavior without making every product
      implementation choice part of the royalty-free public Protocol.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.protocolUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue Protocol →</a><br />
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="agent-readable" class="section">
    <h2>Agent-readable by design</h2>
    <p>
      The documentation states when a verification call applies, when it does not,
      which facts must not be inferred, the possible non-success outcomes, and the
      limits of a successful result. Clean Markdown pages and machine-readable examples
      expose the same underlying boundary to agents and researchers.
    </p>
    <p>
      This documentation helps an agent select and interpret a capability. It does not
      replace the runtime schema, the exact product contract, or the public Protocol.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/docs/">Agent-readable documentation →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="section">
    <h2>What nomue does not claim</h2>
    <p>
      A supported verification result is deliberately narrower than a claim that the
      research is correct. It does not establish:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {CORE_NON_CLAIMS.map((claim) => <li>{claim};</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      The useful property is narrower: each supported check identifies what it checked,
      under which scope and version, and what remains outside the claim. There is no
      single overall VERIFIED result in the public Protocol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p>Current public support: Release 1 Public Draft — experimental.</p>
    <p><a href="/docs/">Next: Docs →</a></p>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-bottom: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .page-head .eyebrow { margin: 0; }
  .page-head h1 { margin: 0.9rem 0 1.25rem; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem); line-height: 1.25; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .standfirst { margin: 0; font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .section { padding: 2.75rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .section h2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .section > :last-child, article > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .sequence, .status-list { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .sequence > div { padding-left: 1.25rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .sequence-label, .status-label { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .call-steps { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 400; }
  .status-list article { padding: 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .status-list h3 { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .links { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .page-foot { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; padding: 2rem 0 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .page-foot p { margin: 0; }
</style>
